{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I’m Dr. ___. You were brought in because of nausea, vomiting, and a very fast heart rate. How are you feeling right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m a bit shaky, but not in pain. My throat hurts a lot, and I’ve been so nauseated since my last radiation. I just couldn’t keep anything down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Your heart rate was in the 180s when you arrived. We gave you medication to slow it down, and now it’s around 130. We’ve moved you to the ICU for close monitoring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is it my cancer? Or the treatment? I always get nauseated after radiation, but this time it’s worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s likely a big part of it. The nausea and poor eating may have led to dehydration, which can trigger a fast heart rate. We’re giving you IV fluids and checking your labs to understand more."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been so weak. I can’t even eat a cracker without feeling sick. And my throat burns when I swallow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We saw on your CT scan that your esophagus is inflamed — that’s probably why it hurts. It could be from radiation or an infection like thrush. We’ll start an antifungal and a special mouthwash to soothe it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need a feeding tube? I’m scared I won’t be able to eat at all."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re not there yet. For now, we’re starting TPN — that’s nutrition through your IV — to help you heal. We’ll keep trying to get you to eat small amounts."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that helps. What about my heart? Is it damaged?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your echocardiogram shows your heart is a bit weak — ejection fraction is 45%, which is lower than normal. But it’s likely from stress and dehydration, not permanent damage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not a heart attack?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, your troponin is normal, so no heart attack. You’re in atrial fibrillation, but it’s new and likely triggered by your current state. We’ll manage it with medication."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m on metoprolol at home. Can I go back to that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2173-10-08 17:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, once your heart rate is stable, we’ll switch you to oral metoprolol. We’ll also restart your lisinopril and amlodipine as your blood pressure allows."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2173-10-09 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs show your white blood cell count is low — 1.8 — so we’re watching for infection. But your blood cultures are clean so far."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2173-10-09 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that from the chemo? I’ve been neutropenic before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2173-10-09 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, very likely. We’ll continue to monitor and keep your environment clean. Your liver enzymes are also elevated, but that’s common with malnutrition and meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2173-10-09 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve lost so much weight. The Boost shakes help, but I can’t taste anything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2173-10-09 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We know. That’s why we started TPN yesterday. It’s giving you protein, calories, and vitamins directly. We’ll keep it going until you can eat more."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your heart rhythm spontaneously converted to normal sinus rhythm last night — great news. We’ve stopped the IV diltiazem and started oral metoprolol."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Really? I didn’t even feel it change."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s common. Since your CHADS score is low and you’re not at high risk for clots, we won’t start blood thinners. But we’ll keep you on metoprolol for rate control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m allergic to aspirin, so I’m glad we’re not adding more meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve noted that. We’ll avoid anything with aspirin or codeine. Your pain is still controlled with morphine?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, but I’d like to switch back to my regular pills when I can eat."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll transition you soon. We’re also treating your esophagitis with fluconazole and a numbing mouthwash. How’s the throat pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little better since the mouthwash. I tried some applesauce last night — didn’t throw it up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2173-10-10 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s progress. Keep small amounts. We’ll keep encouraging oral intake as tolerated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2173-10-11 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We noticed your port wasn’t in the right place on the X-ray. It’s likely why your chemo hasn’t been absorbing well. We’re scheduling a procedure to replace it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2173-10-11 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, that makes sense. I’ve been feeling like the treatments aren’t working."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2173-10-11 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll remove the old one and put in a new port today. It’s a quick procedure, and you’ll be under light sedation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2173-10-11 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The port was successfully replaced. The new one is in the right spot — we confirmed with X-ray. You’ll be able to resume your treatments tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2173-10-11 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel more hopeful already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2173-10-12 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your latest labs show your acidosis has corrected — your bicarbonate is now 32. That means your body’s chemistry is balancing out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2173-10-12 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Was that from not eating?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2173-10-12 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, likely starvation ketoacidosis. With fluids and TPN, your body has what it needs now. Your white count is also improving — up to 3.2."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2173-10-12 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my lungs? I’ve been wheezing a bit."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2173-10-12 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The CT shows patchy opacities — could be fluid or irritation. But no infection or PE. We’ll keep your inhalers going and monitor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2173-10-12 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re receiving your final radiation treatment today. After this, you’ll follow up with your oncologist as an outpatient."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2173-10-12 12:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad it’s the last one. I just want to feel like myself again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2173-10-13 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your breathing is a little tighter today. The CT shows some new patchy areas in your lungs. We’re not sure if it’s fluid from heart strain or a reaction to meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2173-10-13 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Could it be pneumonia?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2173-10-13 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Unlikely — no fever, and your cultures are negative. We’ll watch closely and adjust oxygen if needed. For now, it’s not severe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2173-10-14 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing better. You’ve been eating small meals, your energy is up, and your labs continue to improve. We’re planning for discharge in a day or two."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2173-10-14 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. Will I still need the TPN at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2173-10-14 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not immediately. We’ll transition you to oral nutrition and see how you do. If you struggle, we’ll discuss home TPN later."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2173-10-14 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my meds? I saw some changes listed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2173-10-14 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We’re switching from metoprolol tartrate to metoprolol succinate — it lasts longer. And we’re giving you a liquid oxycodone for pain, easier to swallow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2173-10-14 08:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the mouthwash for my throat?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2173-10-14 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you’ll take that every eight hours for throat pain. Also, keep using the petrolatum on your radiation burns."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2173-10-15 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Final update: your heart is stable, lungs are clearing, and you’ve kept down solid food for two days. We’re discharging you tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2173-10-15 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Home with services, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2173-10-15 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, a nurse will check in, and you’ll follow up with oncology, cardiology, and nutrition. Call us if you have chest pain, trouble breathing, or can’t keep food down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2173-10-15 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for getting me through this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2173-10-16 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re ready to go home. Remember: take your meds, use the mouthwash, eat small frequent meals, and rest. You’ve made great progress."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2173-10-16 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do my best. I just want to get stronger."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2173-10-16 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You will. We’ll see you in follow-up. Take care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2173-10-16 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2173-10-16 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Have a safe trip home."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2173-10-08 16:46:00",
    "admission_end": "2173-10-16 13:05:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "Mrs. ___ was admitted for persistent nausea, vomiting, and poor oral intake following radiation therapy for tracheal cancer, which led to dehydration, metabolic acidosis, and new-onset atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular rate. She was initially managed in the ICU with IV diltiazem for rate control, which was later transitioned to oral metoprolol after spontaneous conversion to sinus rhythm. Her acidosis, likely due to starvation ketoacidosis, resolved with fluid resuscitation and initiation of TPN. Esophagitis, possibly candidal, was treated with fluconazole and a numbing mouthwash. A malpositioned port was identified and replaced to ensure proper delivery of ongoing cancer therapy. She completed her final radiation treatment during admission and showed improvement in oral intake, nutritional status, and overall strength. She was discharged home with services on a revised medication regimen, including metoprolol succinate, continued pain and nausea management, and instructions for wound and nutrition care.",
    "problems": [
      "Atrial fibrillation",
      "Acidosis",
      "Unspecified protein-calorie malnutrition",
      "Malignant neoplasm of trachea",
      "Dysphagia, unspecified",
      "Neutropenia, unspecified",
      "Dehydration",
      "Nausea with vomiting",
      "Esophagitis, unspecified",
      "Mechanical complication of other vascular device, implant, and graft"
    ]
  }
}
